<p id="mwRQ"><b id="mwRg">Tesco plc</b> (<span class="rt-commentedText nowrap" about="#mwt61" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwRw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"IPAc-en","href":"./Template:IPAc-en"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"&apos;"},"2":{"wt":"t"},"3":{"wt":"ɛ"},"4":{"wt":"s"},"5":{"wt":"."},"6":{"wt":"k"},"7":{"wt":"oʊ"}},"i":0}}]}'><span class="IPA nopopups noexcerpt" lang="en-fonipa"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:IPA/English" title="Help:IPA/English">/<span style="border-bottom:1px dotted"><span title="/ˈ/: primary stress follows">ˈ</span><span title="'t' in 'tie'">t</span><span title="/ɛ/: 'e' in 'dress'">ɛ</span><span title="'s' in 'sigh'">s</span><span title="/./: syllable break">.</span><span title="'k' in 'kind'">k</span><span title="/oʊ/: 'o' in 'code'">oʊ</span></span>/</a></span></span>) is a British multinational groceries and general merchandise retailer headquartered in the United Kingdom at its head offices in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Welwyn_Garden_City" title="Welwyn Garden City" id="mwSA">Welwyn Garden City</a>, England.<sup about="#mwt64" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-8"}}'><a href="#cite_note-8" id="mwSQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwSg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwSw">[</span>7<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwTA">]</span></span></a></sup> The company was founded by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Cohen_(businessman)" title="Jack Cohen (businessman)" id="mwTQ">Sir Jack Cohen</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hackney,_London" title="Hackney, London" id="mwTg">Hackney, London</a>, in 1919. In 2023, it was the 15th largest retailer in the world by revenue.<sup about="#mwt67" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-9" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-9"}}'><a href="#cite_note-9" id="mwTw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwUA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUQ">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwUg">]</span></span></a></sup> It is the market leader of groceries in the UK (where it has a market share of around 28.4%).<sup about="#mwt70" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-10" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-10"}}'><a href="#cite_note-10" id="mwUw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwVA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVQ">[</span>9<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwVg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt73" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-11" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-11"}}'><a href="#cite_note-11" id="mwVw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwWA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWQ">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwWg">]</span></span></a></sup> As well as the United Kingdom, Tesco has stores in Czechia, Ireland, Slovakia, the Isle of Man and Hungary.</p>

<p id="mwWw">Since the 1960s, Tesco has <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diversification_(marketing_strategy)" title="Diversification (marketing strategy)" id="mwXA">diversified</a> into areas such as the retailing of books, clothing, electronics, furniture, toys, petrol, software, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_services" title="Financial services" id="mwXQ">financial services</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telecommunications" title="Telecommunications" id="mwXg">telecommunications</a> and internet services. In the 1990s, Tesco re-positioned itself from being a downmarket high-volume low-cost retailer, attempting to attract a range of social groups with its low-cost "Tesco Value" range (launched 1993<sup about="#mwt76" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-BBC_News_12-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BBC News"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-BBC_News-12"}}'><a href="#cite_note-BBC_News-12" id="mwXw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwYA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwYQ">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwYg">]</span></span></a></sup>) and premium "Tesco Finest" range.</p><p id="mwYw">

Tesco is listed on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Stock_Exchange" title="London Stock Exchange" id="mwZA">London Stock Exchange</a> and is a constituent of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FTSE_100_Index" title="FTSE 100 Index" id="mwZQ">FTSE 100 Index</a>.</p><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt77" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwZg" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"TOC limit","href":"./Template:TOC_limit"},"params":{"limit":{"wt":"3"}},"i":0}}]}'><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r886046785" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt78" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Template:TOC limit/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .toclimit-2 .toclevel-1 ul,.mw-parser-output .toclimit-3 .toclevel-2 ul,.mw-parser-output .toclimit-4 .toclevel-3 ul,.mw-parser-output .toclimit-5 .toclevel-4 ul,.mw-parser-output .toclimit-6 .toclevel-5 ul,.mw-parser-output .toclimit-7 .toclevel-6 ul{display:none}</style></span><div class="toclimit-3" about="#mwt77" id="mwZw"><meta property="mw:PageProp/toc"/></div>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="1" id="mwaA" aria-labelledby="History"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id="History">History</h2></div>
<section data-mw-section-id="2" id="mwaQ" aria-labelledby="Origins"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Origins">Origins</h3></div>
<p id="mwag"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Cohen_(businessman)" title="Jack Cohen (businessman)" id="mwaw">Jack Cohen</a>, the son of Jewish migrants from Poland, founded Tesco in 1919 when he began to sell war-surplus groceries from a stall at Well Street Market, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Borough_of_Hackney" title="London Borough of Hackney" id="mwbA">Hackney</a>, in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_End_of_London" title="East End of London" id="mwbQ">East End of London</a>.<sup about="#mwt81" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tescohistsite_13-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tescohistsite"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Tescohistsite-13"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Tescohistsite-13" id="mwbg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwbw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcA">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwcQ">]</span></span></a></sup> The Tesco brand first appeared in 1924. The name came about after Jack Cohen bought a shipment of tea from <i id="mwcg">Thomas Edward Stockwell</i>. He made new labels using the initials of the supplier's name (TES), and the first two letters of his surname (CO), forming the word TESCO.<sup about="#mwt82" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tescohistsite_13-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tescohistsite"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Tescohistsite-13" id="mwcw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwdA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdQ">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwdg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt85" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-14" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-14"}}'><a href="#cite_note-14" id="mwdw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mweA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mweQ">[</span>13<span class="cite-bracket" id="mweg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt88" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-15" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-15"}}'><a href="#cite_note-15" id="mwew"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwfA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfQ">[</span>14<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwfg">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwfw">After experimenting with his first permanent indoor market stall at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tooting" title="Tooting" id="mwgA">Tooting</a> in November 1930, Jack Cohen opened the first Tesco shop in September 1931 at 54 Watling Avenue, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burnt_Oak" title="Burnt Oak" id="mwgQ">Burnt Oak</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edgware" title="Edgware" id="mwgg">Edgware</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Middlesex" title="Middlesex" id="mwgw">Middlesex</a>.<sup about="#mwt89" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Jack_Cohen_16-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Jack Cohen"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Jack_Cohen-16"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Jack_Cohen-16" id="mwhA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwhQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhg">[</span>15<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwhw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt92" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Shirley_Porter_17-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Shirley Porter"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Shirley_Porter-17"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Shirley_Porter-17" id="mwiA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwiQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwig">[</span>16<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwiw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt95" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sarah_Ryle_18-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sarah Ryle"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sarah_Ryle-18"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Sarah_Ryle-18" id="mwjA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwjQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjg">[</span>17<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwjw">]</span></span></a></sup> Tesco was floated on the London Stock Exchange in 1947 as Tesco Stores (Holdings) Limited.<sup about="#mwt96" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tescohistsite_13-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tescohistsite"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Tescohistsite-13" id="mwkA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwkQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkg">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwkw">]</span></span></a></sup> The first self-service shop opened in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Albans" title="St Albans" id="mwlA">St Albans</a> in 1948 (which remained operational until 2010 before relocating to larger premises on the same street, with a period as a Tesco Metro),<sup about="#mwt99" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-19" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-19"}}'><a href="#cite_note-19" id="mwlQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwlg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwlw">[</span>18<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwmA">]</span></span></a></sup> and the first supermarket in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maldon,_Essex" title="Maldon, Essex" class="mw-redirect" id="mwmQ">Maldon</a> in 1956.<sup about="#mwt100" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tescohistsite_13-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tescohistsite"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Tescohistsite-13" id="mwmg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwmw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnA">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwnQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="3" id="mwng" aria-labelledby="Expansion"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Expansion">Expansion</h3></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwnw"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tiverton_,_Tesco_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1287902.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwoA"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tiverton_,_Tesco_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1287902.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Tiverton_%2C_Tesco_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1287902.jpg/250px-Tiverton_%2C_Tesco_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1287902.jpg" decoding="async" data-file-width="640" data-file-height="480" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Tiverton_%2C_Tesco_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1287902.jpg/500px-Tiverton_%2C_Tesco_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1287902.jpg 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwoQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwog">Tesco in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiverton,_Devon" title="Tiverton, Devon" id="mwow">Tiverton, Devon</a>, England, <abbr title="circa" about="#mwt101" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwpA" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Circa","href":"./Template:Circa"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2004"}},"i":0}}]}'>c.</abbr><span style="white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt101" id="mwpQ"><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>2004</span></figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwpg">During the 1950s and 1960s, Tesco grew organically, and also through acquisitions, until it owned more than 800 shops.<sup about="#mwt104" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:0a_20-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":0a"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-:0a-20"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:0a-20" id="mwpw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwqA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqQ">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwqg">]</span></span></a></sup> The company purchased 70 <i id="mwqw">Williamson's</i> shops (1957), 200 <i id="mwrA">Harrow Stores</i> outlets (1959), 212 <i id="mwrQ">Irwins</i> shops (1960), 97 <i id="mwrg">Charles Phillips</i> shops (1964) and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_Value" title="Victor Value" id="mwrw">Victor Value</a> chain (1968) (sold to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bejam" title="Bejam" id="mwsA">Bejam</a> in 1986).<sup about="#mwt105" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:0a_20-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":0a"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:0a-20" id="mwsQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwsg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwsw">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt108" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-21" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-21"}}'><a href="#cite_note-21" id="mwtQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwtg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwtw">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwuA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwuQ">Jack Cohen's business motto was "pile it high and sell it cheap",<sup about="#mwt111" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-YCDBSOYA_1_22-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"YCDBSOYA_1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-YCDBSOYA_1-22"}}'><a href="#cite_note-YCDBSOYA_1-22" id="mwug"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwuw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvA">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwvQ">]</span></span></a></sup> to which he added an internal motto of "YCDBSOYA" (You Can't Do Business Sitting On Your Arse) which he used to motivate his sales force.<sup about="#mwt112" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-YCDBSOYA_1_22-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"YCDBSOYA_1"}}'><a href="#cite_note-YCDBSOYA_1-22" id="mwvg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwvw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwA">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwwQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwwg">In May 1987, Tesco completed its <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hostile_takeover" title="Hostile takeover" class="mw-redirect" id="mwww">hostile takeover</a> of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillards" title="Hillards" id="mwxA">Hillards</a> chain of 40 supermarkets in the North of England for £220<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwxQ"> </span>million.<sup about="#mwt115" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-23" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-23"}}'><a href="#cite_note-23" id="mwxg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwxw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwyA">[</span>22<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwyQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwyg">In 1994, the company took over the supermarket chain <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Low" title="William Low" id="mwyw">William Low</a> after fighting off <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sainsbury's" title="Sainsbury's" id="mwzA">Sainsbury's</a> for control of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dundee" title="Dundee" id="mwzQ">Dundee</a>-based firm, which operated 57 shops. This paved the way for Tesco to expand its presence in Scotland, in which its presence was weaker than in England.<sup about="#mwt118" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-24" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-24"}}'><a href="#cite_note-24" id="mwzg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwzw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0A">[</span>23<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw0Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw0g">Tesco introduced a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loyalty_card" title="Loyalty card" class="mw-redirect" id="mw0w">loyalty card</a>, branded '<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clubcard" title="Clubcard" class="mw-redirect" id="mw1A">Clubcard</a>' in 1995,<sup about="#mwt121" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-25" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-25"}}'><a href="#cite_note-25" id="mw1Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw1g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw1w">[</span>24<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2A">]</span></span></a></sup> and later an Internet shopping service. In 1996, the typeface of the logo was changed to the current version with stripe reflections underneath, whilst the corporate font used for shop signage was changed from the familiar "typewriter" font that had been used since the 1970s. Overseas operations were introduced in the same year.<sup about="#mwt122" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-BBC_News_12-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"BBC News"}}'><a href="#cite_note-BBC_News-12" id="mw2Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw2g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw2w">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw3A">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terry_Leahy" title="Terry Leahy" id="mw3Q">Terry Leahy</a> assumed the role of Chief Executive in February 1997.<sup about="#mwt125" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-26" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-26"}}'><a href="#cite_note-26" id="mw3g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw3w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4A">[</span>25<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw4Q">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt128" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-27" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-27"}}'><a href="#cite_note-27" id="mw4g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw4w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5A">[</span>26<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw5Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw5g">In March 1997, Tesco purchased the retail arm of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_British_Foods" title="Associated British Foods" id="mw5w">Associated British Foods</a>, which consisted of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quinnsworth" title="Quinnsworth" id="mw6A">Quinnsworth</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stewarts_Supermarkets" title="Stewarts Supermarkets" class="mw-redirect" id="mw6Q">Stewarts</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crazy_Prices" title="Crazy Prices" class="mw-redirect" id="mw6g">Crazy Prices</a> chains in Ireland and Northern Ireland, and associated businesses, for £640<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mw6w"> </span>million.<sup about="#mwt131" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-28" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-28"}}'><a href="#cite_note-28" id="mw7A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw7Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7g">[</span>27<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw7w">]</span></span></a></sup> The deal was approved by the European Commission in May 1997.<sup about="#mwt134" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-29" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-29"}}'><a href="#cite_note-29" id="mw8A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw8Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8g">[</span>28<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw8w">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mw9A">The company was the subject of a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesco_bomb_campaign" title="Tesco bomb campaign" id="mw9Q">letter bomb campaign</a> lasting five months from August 2000 to February 2001, as a bomber calling himself "Sally" sent letter bombs to Tesco customers and demanded that Clubcards be modified to be capable of withdrawing money from cash machines.<sup about="#mwt138" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-30" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-30"}}'><a href="#cite_note-30" id="mw9g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mw9w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-A">[</span>29<span class="cite-bracket" id="mw-Q">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="4" id="mw-g" aria-labelledby="Diversification"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="Diversification">Diversification</h3></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mw-w"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_Tomaszów_Mazowiecki.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mw_A"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_Tomaszów_Mazowiecki.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Tesco_Tomasz%C3%B3w_Mazowiecki.jpg/250px-Tesco_Tomasz%C3%B3w_Mazowiecki.jpg" decoding="async" data-file-width="2560" data-file-height="1536" data-file-type="bitmap" height="150" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Tesco_Tomasz%C3%B3w_Mazowiecki.jpg/500px-Tesco_Tomasz%C3%B3w_Mazowiecki.jpg 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mw_Q"/></a><figcaption id="mw_g">Tesco in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomaszów_Mazowiecki" title="Tomaszów Mazowiecki" id="mw_w">Tomaszów Mazowiecki</a>, Poland, 2018</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwAQA">In the 1960s, Tesco expanded the range of products it sold to include household goods and clothing under the Delamare brand, and in 1974 opened its first petrol station.<sup about="#mwt141" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-31" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-31"}}'><a href="#cite_note-31" id="mwAQE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQM">[</span>30<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQQ">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt142" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tescohistsite_13-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tescohistsite"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Tescohistsite-13" id="mwAQU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQc">[</span>12<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt145" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-32" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-32"}}'><a href="#cite_note-32" id="mwAQk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQs">[</span>31<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAQw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAQ0">In 2001, Tesco became involved in internet grocery retailing in the US when it obtained a 35% stake in GroceryWorks.<sup about="#mwt148" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-33" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-33"}}'><a href="#cite_note-33" id="mwAQ4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAQ8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARA">[</span>32<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwARI">In 2002, Tesco purchased 13 HIT hypermarkets in Poland. It made a major move into the UK's convenience shop market with its purchase of T&amp;S Stores, owner of 870 convenience shops in the One Stop, Dillons, and Day &amp; Nite chains in the UK.<sup about="#mwt151" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-34" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-34"}}'><a href="#cite_note-34" id="mwARM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARU">[</span>33<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwARc">In June 2003, Tesco purchased the C Two-Network in Japan.<sup about="#mwt154" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-35" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-35"}}'><a href="#cite_note-35" id="mwARg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwARk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARo">[</span>34<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwARs">]</span></span></a></sup> It acquired a majority stake in the Turkish supermarket chain <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesco_Kipa" title="Tesco Kipa" class="mw-redirect" id="mwARw">Kipa</a>.<sup about="#mwt157" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-36" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-36"}}'><a href="#cite_note-36" id="mwAR0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAR4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAR8">[</span>35<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASA">]</span></span></a></sup> In January 2004, Tesco acquired Adminstore, owner of 45 Cullens, Europa, and Harts convenience shops, in and around London.<sup about="#mwt160" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-37" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-37"}}'><a href="#cite_note-37" id="mwASE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASM">[</span>36<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwASU">In Thailand, Tesco Lotus was a joint venture of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charoen_Pokphand" title="Charoen Pokphand" id="mwASY">Charoen Pokphand</a> Group and Tesco but, facing criticism over the growth of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypermarket" title="Hypermarket" id="mwASc">hypermarkets</a>, CP Group sold its Tesco Lotus shares in 2003.<sup about="#mwt163" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-38" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-38"}}'><a href="#cite_note-38" id="mwASg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwASk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASo">[</span>37<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwASs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwASw">In late 2005 Tesco acquired the 21 remaining <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safeway_(UK)" title="Safeway (UK)" id="mwAS0">Safeway</a>/<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BP" title="BP" id="mwAS4">BP</a> shops after <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morrisons" title="Morrisons" id="mwAS8">Morrisons</a> dissolved the Safeway/BP partnership.<sup about="#mwt166" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-39" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-39"}}'><a href="#cite_note-39" id="mwATA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATI">[</span>38<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwATQ">In 2006, Tesco announced plans to move into the United States by opening a chain of small-format groceries in the Western states (Arizona, California, and Nevada) in 2007 named <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fresh_&amp;_Easy" title="Fresh &amp; Easy" id="mwATU">Fresh &amp; Easy</a>. Tesco pulled out of the United States market in 2013, following performance issues.<sup about="#mwt169" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-corp2006_40-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"corp2006"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-corp2006-40"}}'><a href="#cite_note-corp2006-40" id="mwATY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATg">[</span>39<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="5" id="mwATo" aria-labelledby="2010s"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="2010s">2010s</h3></div>
<p id="mwATs">In 2010, Tesco started funding a small film studio intended to produce Tesco-exclusive direct-to-DVD films. The first film was released on 6 September called <i id="mwATw"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_Connections" title="Paris Connections" id="mwAT0">Paris Connections</a></i>, based on a popular novel by Jackie Collins.<sup about="#mwt172" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-41" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-41"}}'><a href="#cite_note-41" id="mwAT4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAT8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUA">[</span>40<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt175" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-42" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-42"}}'><a href="#cite_note-42" id="mwAUI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUQ">[</span>41<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAUY">In 2013, Tesco pulled out of its US market (Fresh &amp; Easy) stores in April, after it filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy, at a reported cost of £1.2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAUc"> </span>billion.<sup about="#mwt178" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-43" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-43"}}'><a href="#cite_note-43" id="mwAUg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAUk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUo">[</span>42<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAUs">]</span></span></a></sup> In September, Tesco announced that it would sell the business to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Burkle" title="Ronald Burkle" id="mwAUw">Ronald Burkle</a>'s <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yucaipa_Companies" title="Yucaipa Companies" id="mwAU0">Yucaipa Companies</a> for an undisclosed amount.<sup about="#mwt181" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-44" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-44"}}'><a href="#cite_note-44" id="mwAU4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAU8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVA">[</span>43<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVE">]</span></span></a></sup> Tesco also purchased the restaurant and cafe chain Giraffe for £48.6<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAVI"> </span>million.<sup about="#mwt184" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-45" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-45"}}'><a href="#cite_note-45" id="mwAVM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAVQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVU">[</span>44<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAVY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAVc"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_supermarket_trollies_(detail).jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAVg"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_supermarket_trollies_(detail).jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tesco_supermarket_trollies_%28detail%29.jpg/250px-Tesco_supermarket_trollies_%28detail%29.jpg" decoding="async" data-file-width="4000" data-file-height="3000" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tesco_supermarket_trollies_%28detail%29.jpg/500px-Tesco_supermarket_trollies_%28detail%29.jpg 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAVk"/></a><figcaption id="mwAVo">Tesco trollies</figcaption></figure> 
<p id="mwAVs">In 2015, Tesco sold its <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blinkbox" title="Blinkbox" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAVw">Blinkbox</a> on-demand video service and its fixed-line telephone and broadband business to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TalkTalk_Group" title="TalkTalk Group" id="mwAV0">TalkTalk</a>.<sup about="#mwt187" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-independent.co.uk_46-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"independent.co.uk"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-independent.co.uk-46"}}'><a href="#cite_note-independent.co.uk-46" id="mwAV4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAV8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWA">[</span>45<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWE">]</span></span></a></sup> In January, Tesco sold the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blinkbox_Music" title="Blinkbox Music" id="mwAWI">Blinkbox Music</a> streaming service to now-defunct <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guvera" title="Guvera" id="mwAWM">Guvera</a>,<sup about="#mwt190" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-47" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-47"}}'><a href="#cite_note-47" id="mwAWQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWY">[</span>46<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWc">]</span></span></a></sup> and confirmed it would close its Blinkbox Books service by the end of February.<sup about="#mwt194" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-48" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-48"}}'><a href="#cite_note-48" id="mwAWg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAWk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWo">[</span>47<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAWs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAWw">In 2016, Tesco confirmed it was seeking to sell <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dobbies_Garden_Centres" title="Dobbies Garden Centres" id="mwAW0">Dobbies Garden Centres</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giraffe_Restaurants" title="Giraffe Restaurants" class="mw-redirect" id="mwAW4">Giraffe Restaurants</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harris_+_Hoole" title="Harris + Hoole" id="mwAW8">Harris + Hoole</a> to concentrate on its main supermarket business.<sup about="#mwt197" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-49" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-49"}}'><a href="#cite_note-49" id="mwAXA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXI">[</span>48<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAXQ">In 2017, Tesco reached an agreement to merge with Britain's biggest wholesaler <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker_Group" title="Booker Group" id="mwAXU">Booker Group</a>. There were concerns over market dominance with Tesco being Britain's largest food retailer and Booker being the UK's largest wholesaler.<sup about="#mwt200" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-50" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-50"}}'><a href="#cite_note-50" id="mwAXY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXg">[</span>49<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAXk">]</span></span></a></sup> In April, the company sold its in-shop opticians' business to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vision_Express" title="Vision Express" id="mwAXo">Vision Express</a>.<sup about="#mwt203" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-51" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-51"}}'><a href="#cite_note-51" id="mwAXs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAXw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAX0">[</span>50<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAX4">]</span></span></a></sup> In June, Tesco announced a major cost-cutting initiative that would reduce the company's workforce by over 1,200 workers. Key reductions included over a quarter of its employees in Welwyn Garden City and Hatfield, and the closure of the call centre in Cardiff. The company hoped to reduce costs by £1.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwAX8"> </span>billion.<sup about="#mwt206" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-52" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-52"}}'><a href="#cite_note-52" id="mwAYA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYI">[</span>51<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAYQ">In 2019, Tesco announced another cost-cutting initiative that would close the food counters in 90 stores, affecting around 9,000 workers.<sup about="#mwt209" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-53" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-53"}}'><a href="#cite_note-53" id="mwAYU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYc">[</span>52<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYg">]</span></span></a></sup> In October 2019, Tesco announced that CEO Dave Lewis would step down in 2020,<sup about="#mwt212" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-54" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-54"}}'><a href="#cite_note-54" id="mwAYk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAYo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYs">[</span>53<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAYw">]</span></span></a></sup> and would be succeeded by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Murphy_(businessman)" title="Ken Murphy (businessman)" id="mwAY0">Ken Murphy</a>.<sup about="#mwt215" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Guardian_55-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Guardian"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Guardian-55"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Guardian-55" id="mwAY4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAY8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZA">[</span>54<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="6" id="mwAZI" aria-labelledby="2020s"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id="2020s">2020s</h3></div><p id="mwAZM">
In February 2020, Tesco announced the sale of its 20% stake in its Chinese joint venture with China Resources for £257 million. The joint venture, created in 2014, had taken over all of Tesco’s stores in China.<sup about="#mwt218" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-56" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-56"}}'><a href="#cite_note-56" id="mwAZQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZY">[</span>55<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZc">]</span></span></a></sup> In March 2020, Tesco announced the sale of its operations in Thailand and Malaysia, comprising 2,000 stores (including Tesco Lotus supermarkets), to Charoen Pokphand Group for $10 billion.<sup about="#mwt221" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-57" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-57"}}'><a href="#cite_note-57" id="mwAZg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZo">[</span>56<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZs">]</span></span></a></sup> In June 2020, Tesco announced the sale of its operations in Poland, comprising nearly 300 stores, to Salling Group for £165 million.<sup about="#mwt224" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-58" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-58"}}'><a href="#cite_note-58" id="mwAZw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAZ0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZ4">[</span>57<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAZ8">]</span></span></a></sup></p><figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAaA"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_international_operations.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAaE"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_international_operations.svg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Tesco_international_operations.svg/250px-Tesco_international_operations.svg.png" decoding="async" data-file-width="2754" data-file-height="1398" data-file-type="drawing" height="127" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Tesco_international_operations.svg/500px-Tesco_international_operations.svg.png 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAaI"/></a><figcaption id="mwAaM">Countries served by Tesco stores:<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt225" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAaQ" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Legend","href":"./Template:Legend"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"#2171b5"},"2":{"wt":"Currently operating"}},"i":0}}]}'><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r981673959" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt226" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Legend/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .legend{page-break-inside:avoid;break-inside:avoid-column}.mw-parser-output .legend-color{display:inline-block;min-width:1.25em;height:1.25em;line-height:1.25;margin:1px 0;text-align:center;border:1px solid black;background-color:transparent;color:black}.mw-parser-output .legend-text{}</style></span><div class="legend" about="#mwt225" id="mwAaU"><span class="legend-color mw-no-invert" style="background-color:#2171b5; color:white;-webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact;"><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Currently operating</div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt227" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwAaY" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Legend","href":"./Template:Legend"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"#bdd7e7"},"2":{"wt":"Previously operated"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r981673959" about="#mwt228" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Legend/styles.css"}}'/></span><div class="legend" about="#mwt227" id="mwAac"><span class="legend-color mw-no-invert" style="background-color:#bdd7e7; color:black;-webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact;"><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Previously operated</div></figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwAag">In 2023, Tesco acquired all nine <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shoprite_(Isle_of_Man)" title="Shoprite (Isle of Man)" id="mwAak">Shoprite</a> shops on the Isle of Man from the Nicholson family who had built the chain up since 1972.<sup about="#mwt231" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-59" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-59"}}'><a href="#cite_note-59" id="mwAao"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAas"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAaw">[</span>58<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAa0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAa4">In February 2024, Barclays announced the acquisition of most of Tesco's banking operations for £600 million, along with the signing of a 10-year partnership between the two companies.<sup about="#mwt234" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-60" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-60"}}'><a href="#cite_note-60" id="mwAa8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbE">[</span>59<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwAbM">It supports LGBTQ+ charities and organizations, and has made substantial donations to them. It regularly appears in Pride marches.<sup about="#mwt237" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-61" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-61"}}'><a href="#cite_note-61" id="mwAbQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwAbU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbY">[</span>60<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwAbc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAbg"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAbk"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco.jpg/250px-Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco.jpg" decoding="async" data-file-width="4080" data-file-height="3060" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco.jpg/500px-Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco.jpg 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAbo"/></a><figcaption id="mwAbs">Manchester Pride 2025 Tesco</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwAbw"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco_marchers.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwAb0"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco_marchers.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco_marchers.jpg/250px-Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco_marchers.jpg" decoding="async" data-file-width="4080" data-file-height="3060" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco_marchers.jpg/500px-Manchester_Pride_2025_Tesco_marchers.jpg 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwAb4"/></a><figcaption id="mwAb8">Manchester Pride 2025 Tesco marchers</figcaption></figure>

