<section data-mw-section-id="35" id="mwBBE" aria-labelledby="Czech_Republic"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Czech_Republic">Czech Republic</h4></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBBI"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_express_prague_-_belehradska_street.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBBM"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_express_prague_-_belehradska_street.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Tesco_express_prague_-_belehradska_street.jpg/250px-Tesco_express_prague_-_belehradska_street.jpg" decoding="async" data-file-width="768" data-file-height="623" data-file-type="bitmap" height="203" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Tesco_express_prague_-_belehradska_street.jpg/500px-Tesco_express_prague_-_belehradska_street.jpg 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBBQ"/></a><figcaption id="mwBBU">A local Tesco Express, known as Tesco Expres in Belehradska Street, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prague" title="Prague" id="mwBBY">Prague</a>, Czech Republic</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBBc">In 1996, Tesco expanded to the Czech Republic when they purchased <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kmart" title="Kmart" id="mwBBg">Kmart</a>'s local operations for $117.5 million and rebranded the stores as Tesco.<sup about="#mwt466" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-nyt-1996mar06_122-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"nyt-1996mar06"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-nyt-1996mar06-122"}}'><a href="#cite_note-nyt-1996mar06-122" id="mwBBk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBBo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBs">[</span>121<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBBw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt469" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ct-1996mar05_123-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ct-1996mar05"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-ct-1996mar05-123"}}'><a href="#cite_note-ct-1996mar05-123" id="mwBB0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBB4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBB8">[</span>122<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCA">]</span></span></a></sup> In December 2005, Tesco expanded its operations in the country by purchasing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrefour" title="Carrefour" id="mwBCE">Carrefour</a>'s local operations.<sup about="#mwt472" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-124" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-124"}}'><a href="#cite_note-124" id="mwBCI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCQ">[</span>123<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBCY">Tesco has been a market leader within the Czech Republic and is one of the largest supermarket chains in the country, operating over 322 stores by 2012,<sup about="#mwt475" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-125" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-125"}}'><a href="#cite_note-125" id="mwBCc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCk">[</span>124<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBCo">]</span></span></a></sup> upwards of 300 by 2007.<sup about="#mwt478" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-autogenerated1_126-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"autogenerated1"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-autogenerated1-126"}}'><a href="#cite_note-autogenerated1-126" id="mwBCs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBCw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBC0">[</span>125<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBC4">]</span></span></a></sup> Tesco is keen to expand non-food items and has already opened petrol stations and offers personal finance services in the Czech Republic.<sup about="#mwt481" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-127" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-127"}}'><a href="#cite_note-127" id="mwBC8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDE">[</span>126<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBDI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="36" id="mwBDM" aria-labelledby="Hungary"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Hungary">Hungary</h4></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBDQ"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_Kőszeg.JPG" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBDU"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_Kőszeg.JPG" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Tesco_K%C5%91szeg.JPG/250px-Tesco_K%C5%91szeg.JPG" decoding="async" data-file-width="1280" data-file-height="960" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Tesco_K%C5%91szeg.JPG/500px-Tesco_K%C5%91szeg.JPG 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBDY"/></a><figcaption id="mwBDc">Tesco store at <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kőszeg" title="Kőszeg" id="mwBDg">Kőszeg</a>, Hungary with Statue of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fire_Salamander" title="Fire Salamander" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBDk">Fire Salamander</a>, 2011</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBDo">In 1994, Tesco entered Hungary when the company purchased the Győr-based chain S-Market.<sup about="#mwt484" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-128" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-128"}}'><a href="#cite_note-128" id="mwBDs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBDw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD0">[</span>127<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBD4">]</span></span></a></sup> Currently, Tesco operates through more than 200 stores in Hungary with further openings planned.<sup about="#mwt485" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-autogenerated1_126-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"autogenerated1"}}'><a href="#cite_note-autogenerated1-126" id="mwBD8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEE">[</span>125<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEI">]</span></span></a></sup> Tesco Hungary also offers a clothing line and personal finance services.<sup about="#mwt488" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-129" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-129"}}'><a href="#cite_note-129" id="mwBEM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBEQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEU">[</span>128<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBEY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="37" id="mwBEc" aria-labelledby="Slovakia"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Slovakia">Slovakia</h4></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwBEg"><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_Kamenné_námestie.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwBEk"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Tesco_Kamenné_námestie.jpg" src="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Tesco_Kamenn%C3%A9_n%C3%A1mestie.jpg/250px-Tesco_Kamenn%C3%A9_n%C3%A1mestie.jpg" decoding="async" data-file-width="2592" data-file-height="1944" data-file-type="bitmap" height="188" width="250" srcset="//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Tesco_Kamenn%C3%A9_n%C3%A1mestie.jpg/500px-Tesco_Kamenn%C3%A9_n%C3%A1mestie.jpg 2x" class="mw-file-element" id="mwBEo"/></a><figcaption id="mwBEs">Tesco in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bratislava" title="Bratislava" id="mwBEw">Bratislava</a>, Slovakia, 2008</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwBE0">In 1996, Tesco entered the Slovak market by purchasing <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kmart" title="Kmart" id="mwBE4">Kmart</a>'s local operations.<sup about="#mwt489" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-nyt-1996mar06_122-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"nyt-1996mar06"}}'><a href="#cite_note-nyt-1996mar06-122" id="mwBE8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFE">[</span>121<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt490" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-ct-1996mar05_123-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"ct-1996mar05"}}'><a href="#cite_note-ct-1996mar05-123" id="mwBFM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFU">[</span>122<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBFc">Tesco Slovakia caused controversy amongst the Slovak government when it was found to have come foul of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Food_safety_laws" title="Food safety laws" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBFg">food safety laws</a> in 2006.<sup about="#mwt493" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-130" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-130"}}'><a href="#cite_note-130" id="mwBFk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBFo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFs">[</span>129<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBFw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBF0">In April 2010 the first Tesco Extra in Central Europe opened in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bratislava" title="Bratislava" id="mwBF4">Bratislava</a> – <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petržalka" title="Petržalka" id="mwBF8">Petržalka</a>, Slovakia as part of a pilot project for Tesco in the region, including the first self-service cash flow in Central Europe. There are currently seven Tesco Extra stores in Slovakia – three in Bratislava and one each in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zvolen" title="Zvolen" id="mwBGA">Zvolen</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trnava" title="Trnava" id="mwBGE">Trnava</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banská_Bystrica" title="Banská Bystrica" id="mwBGI">Banská Bystrica</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spišská_Nová_Ves" title="Spišská Nová Ves" id="mwBGM">Spišská Nová Ves</a>.<sup about="#mwt496" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-131" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-131"}}'><a href="#cite_note-131" id="mwBGQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBGU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGY">[</span>130<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt499" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-132" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-132"}}'><a href="#cite_note-132" id="mwBGg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBGk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGo">[</span>131<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBGs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="38" id="mwBGw" aria-labelledby="Ireland"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Ireland">Ireland</h4></div>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt500" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBG0" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Tesco Ireland"}},"i":0}}]}'><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1353705441" about="#mwt501" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt500" id="mwBG4">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tesco_Ireland" title="Tesco Ireland">Tesco Ireland</a></div>

<p id="mwBG8">In the early 1980s, Tesco first operated in the Irish grocery market, selling its operations there in March 1986.<sup about="#mwt504" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-133" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-133"}}'><a href="#cite_note-133" id="mwBHA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHI">[</span>132<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHM">]</span></span></a></sup> In 1997, Tesco re-entered the Irish market after the purchase of Power Supermarkets Ltd, which operated the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quinnsworth" title="Quinnsworth" id="mwBHQ">Quinnsworth</a> chain of supermarkets.<sup about="#mwt507" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-134" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-134"}}'><a href="#cite_note-134" id="mwBHU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHc">[</span>133<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt510" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-135" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-135"}}'><a href="#cite_note-135" id="mwBHk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBHo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHs">[</span>134<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBHw">]</span></span></a></sup> It now operates from 154 stores across Ireland. Like Tesco stores in the UK, these offer a home delivery shopping service available to 80% of the Irish population as well as petrol, mobile telephone, personal finance, flower delivery service, and a weight-loss programme.<sup about="#mwt513" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-136" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-136"}}'><a href="#cite_note-136" id="mwBH0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBH4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBH8">[</span>135<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIA">]</span></span></a></sup> Tesco's loyalty programme, Clubcard, is offered in Ireland. Tesco had approximately 21% of the Irish grocery market in 2019 and its main competitors are <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dunnes_Stores" title="Dunnes Stores" id="mwBIE">Dunnes Stores</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SuperValu_(Ireland)" title="SuperValu (Ireland)" id="mwBII">SuperValu</a>.<sup about="#mwt516" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-137" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-137"}}'><a href="#cite_note-137" id="mwBIM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBIQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIU">[</span>136<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBIY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBIc">Tesco Ireland claims to be the largest purchaser of Irish food, with an estimated €1.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwBIg"> </span>billion annually. Tesco Ireland operates a number of Tesco Extra hypermarkets in Ireland, with Clarehall Extra on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R107_road" title="R107 road" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBIk">Malahide Road</a> being the first to open in 2006. In November 2010, Tesco's largest hypermarket store in Europe opened in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dundalk" title="Dundalk" id="mwBIo">Dundalk</a> in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_Louth" title="County Louth" id="mwBIs">County Louth</a>, with a floorspace of <span about="#mwt517" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBIw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"convert","href":"./Template:Convert"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"18500"},"2":{"wt":"m2"},"3":{"wt":"sqft"},"abbr":{"wt":"on"}},"i":0}}]}'>18,500</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt517"> </span><span about="#mwt517">m</span><sup about="#mwt517">2</sup><span about="#mwt517"> (199,000</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt517"> </span><span about="#mwt517">sq</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt517"> </span><span about="#mwt517">ft)</span>.<sup about="#mwt520" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-138" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-138"}}'><a href="#cite_note-138" id="mwBI0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBI4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBI8">[</span>137<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBJE">In April 2011, the <i id="mwBJI"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irish_Times" title="Irish Times" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBJM">Irish Times</a></i> said that "Increasingly, Ireland is being viewed as a provincial backwater by the parent company – albeit a very profitable little backwater – and all the strategic decisions are being taken in the UK.<sup about="#mwt523" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-139" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-139"}}'><a href="#cite_note-139" id="mwBJQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJY">[</span>138<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwBJg">In 2008, Tesco opened its first eco store in Tramore, County Waterford. It is expected to use 45% less energy than other Tesco supermarkets of similar size.<sup about="#mwt526" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-140" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-140"}}'><a href="#cite_note-140" id="mwBJk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBJo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJs">[</span>139<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBJw">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="39" id="mwBJ0" aria-labelledby="Spain,_Portugal,_and_Gibraltar"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Spain,_Portugal,_and_Gibraltar"><span id="Spain.2C_Portugal.2C_and_Gibraltar" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Spain, Portugal, and Gibraltar</h4></div>
<p id="mwBJ4">Tesco supplies six stores in Spain, Portugal, and Gibraltar that operate under the name "The Food Co.". Operations started in 2019 with the first store opening in Puerto de Mazarrón located in the Murcia province of Spain. One other store opened in the Algarve in 2019, followed by four more in 2020, in Gibraltar, Ibiza, Mijas (Málaga), and Quesada (Alicante). All 7,500 products stocked originate from Britain. The Web site stated in 2020 "With more stores coming in Spain &amp; Portugal", but had not been updated <span about="#mwt527" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwBJ8" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"As of","href":"./Template:As_of"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"2023"},"2":{"wt":"9"},"lc":{"wt":"y"}},"i":0}}]}'>as of September</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt527"> </span><span about="#mwt527">2023</span><sup class="plainlinks noexcerpt noprint asof-tag update" style="display:none;" about="#mwt527"><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Tesco&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Tesco&amp;action=edit"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span>update<span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></a></sup><span typeof="mw:Nowiki" about="#mwt527"></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements_from_September_2023" about="#mwt527"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_containing_potentially_dated_statements" about="#mwt527" id="mwBKA"/>.<sup about="#mwt530" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-141" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-141"}}'><a href="#cite_note-141" id="mwBKE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKM">[</span>140<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="40" id="mwBKU" aria-labelledby="India"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="India">India</h4></div>
<p id="mwBKY">Tesco has had a limited presence in India with a service centre in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bangalore" title="Bangalore" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBKc">Bangalore</a>, and outsourcing.<sup about="#mwt533" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-fe2010_142-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"fe2010"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-fe2010-142"}}'><a href="#cite_note-fe2010-142" id="mwBKg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBKk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKo">[</span>141<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBKs">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2008, Tesco announced its intention to invest an initial £60m (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US$" title="US$" class="mw-redirect" id="mwBKw">US$</a>115m) to open a wholesale cash-and-carry business based in Mumbai with the assistance of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tata_Group" title="Tata Group" id="mwBK0">Tata Group</a>.<sup about="#mwt536" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-tata2008_143-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"tata2008"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-tata2008-143"}}'><a href="#cite_note-tata2008-143" id="mwBK4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBK8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLA">[</span>142<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLE">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2014, the joint venture between Tesco and Tata was confirmed, where investment by the earlier was reportedly 140 million dollars, becoming the first foreign supermarket to enter India. The stores operate under the banner Star Bazaar and Star Daily supermarkets.<sup about="#mwt539" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-144" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-144"}}'><a href="#cite_note-144" id="mwBLI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLQ">[</span>143<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLU">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="41" id="mwBLY" aria-labelledby="Pakistan"><div class="mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id="Pakistan">Pakistan</h4></div>

<p id="mwBLc">In February 2017 Tesco announced a wholesale partnership with Limestone Private Limited, owner of the Alpha Superstores chain. This involved an exclusive partnership which would see Tesco products stocked across Alpha Supermarket stores within Pakistan.<sup about="#mwt542" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-145" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-145"}}'><a href="#cite_note-145" id="mwBLg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwBLk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLo">[</span>144<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwBLs">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

